Summary\
\
In this chapter, you learned about virtualization and cloud computing. Virtualization removes the barrier of there being a one-to-one relationship between computer hardware and an operating system. You learned the purpose of virtual machines, which includes sandboxing, test development and application virtualization for legacy software and OSs and cross-platform virtualization, as well as requirements for desktop virtualization. You also learned about containers and the core piece of virtualization software, called the hypervisor. The section finished with a long exercise on installing a hypervisor and Lubuntu on a Windows computer.\
	The next topic was cloud computing, which is virtualization in large scale over the Internet. Cloud computing has been one of the hottest topic in IT circles for several years now and will likely continue to be so for several more years. There are different types of clouds, such as public, private, community, and hybrid. Cloud providers sell several different types of services, such as IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS. Cloud features include shared resources, metered utilization, rapid elasticity, high availability, file synchronization, and multi tenancy. You can use cloud services for storage, virtual applications (such as email or word processing), or both.\
\
Exam Essentials\
\
Understand the purpose of virtual machines.\
	Virtual machines are designed to save providers and users money. They allow for multiple OSs to be installed on one computer. VMs can provide sandboxing, test development, and application virtualization for legacy software and OSs and cross-platform virtualization. \
\
Understand virtual machine requirements\
	A virtual machine has security, network, and storage requirements. Those requirements will tie back to physical resources available on a server somewhere.\
\
Understand what virtual desktops are. \
	A virtual desktop is the collection of software and hardware needed to create a virtual environment. Sometimes it\'92s called a virtual desktop infrastructer (VDI)\
\
Know what the hypervisor is, and the difference between Type 1 and Type 2 hypervisors. \
	The hypervisor is the software which allows the creation of virtual machines on a computer. A type 1 (or bare-metal) hypervisor sits directly on the hardware and does not need a separate OS. This is mostly used for server-side virtualization. A Type 2 hypervisor sits on top of a host OS. \
\
Know what containers are. \
	Containers are an App and the required files (such as OS files and dependencies) needed to run the app in any environment\
\
Understand the four different types of cloud\
	A cloud can be public, private, hybrid, or community.\
\
Know the difference between IaaS, SaaS, and PaaS.\
	All of these are cloud terms. In infrastructure as a service (IaaS), the provider supplies the network infrastructure. In platform as a service (PaaS), software development tools and platforms are also provided. The highest level is software as a service (SaaS), where the provider supplies the infrastructure and applications. 	\
Understand shared resources versus dedicated resources.\
	All clouds use shared resources, which can be internal or external. A pool of resources is purchased and each participant in the cloud pays for part of it. The resources are shared resources. Dedicated resources would be wholly owned by one company.\
\
\
Understand metered utilization, including ingress and egress.\
	Metered utilization shows how much a client has used and will be billed for. Ingress is loading data to the cloud ( which is usually free), and egress is downloading data from the cloud (which may come with an extra cost).\
\
Know what elasticity and availability mean in the cloud. \
	Elasticity means that a client can quickly get more (or fewer) resources as needed. Availability means that services are always or almost always available, such as three nines five.\
\
\
Know what file synchronization means in the cloud.\
	Synchronization means that an exact copy of the file is in multiple locations. Cloud file storage services include iCloud  and Google Cloud, and in most cases have synchronization apps to sync to the mobile device.\
\
Understand multitienancy.\
	Multiple clients can use the same hardware and resources from a single cloud provider, which is called multi tenancy.\
\
